ror effectively that's 42 metres in  diameter which as you might imagine it's  going to collect an absolute shedload of  light so we're gonna be singing able to  see things which we stand no chance at  all of seeing at the moment we'll be  able to see planets like the earth  orbiting around other nearby stars and  even be able to study the atmospheres of  those planets to figure out whether  there's life on them we'll be able to  see stars in other galaxies where at the  moment we can just see kind of the fuzzy  blur of light we're going to be able to  see galaxies on the other side of the  universe on the kind of accuracy where  currently we can only see nearby  galaxies so it's going to completely  revolutionize astronomy 42 metres 42  metres should we should we try so one  easy measurement I have is the computer  that goes on my bike so we're going to  go outside and I'm gonna get on my bike  and see if we can measure approximately  42 metres somewhere in the parking lot  and that's the sad thing these poor  photons of light they've been traveling  across the Gulf of space for you know 10  years hoping some astronomers going to  catch them and they you know they run  the splat into the roof of your house  and that's it it's all over so we'll  catch some of them I mean even with a  mirror 42 metres in diameter we're still  not going to catch very many of them but  it's a much bigger bucket than we've had  in the past so we're gonna come collect  a lot more light than we ever have  before okay so I've got my measuring  instrument here which is my bike and I  have a little wireless bike computer  that just sits on here so I'm gonna  reset that now and we're going to try to  see if we can figure out just how big 42  meters actually is that's still it's an  interesting question because obviously  there are kind of astronomical reasons  as to why you might want to put in  particular places for example there's  some things you can only see from the  southern hemisphere 